#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161907 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162094 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f930dfb2-a688-5dbe-9233-09dbcd2ba383" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sample Mean of an Observation Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "5f949e10-0d73-50c8-9f41-73a0d0f78e29" :question/math-academy-id "e-9833" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1fd70958028ae2c12be1df5808616421da9bb5758999237031a143e48d8e167.svg)\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{x}_i,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_n$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n![](images/f9/f97a4bc5d9323be573cc78cca322502d86d4c890d46b7116ffa1fac87e291672.svg)\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161908 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc84d72a-b9f3-5f99-aa23-31cfe6910d49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Covariance Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "bd9fe016-cd5f-5e65-b388-35725ac0fbf4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9834" :question/problem "![](images/44/44fa46a2d63c5c59dcc95df4132bdfa56400989234b44d2a382eaa2e51d5713b.svg)\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} B B^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n![](images/91/91d73b98e8b008382c1ef311fa262b1c2f0287d6ec64e3e86bd4734304ada251.svg)\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 50\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161909 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e91708c9-67e2-5228-84fd-1f4ea60064ac" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mean-Deviation Form of an Observation Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "bdef2fdc-0a38-5bfa-9bc6-7ca50e8c10ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-9835" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9617e549830347cdd014ce812011ed679a95c576ea2920a93fdc4f09a824b55.svg)\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n![](images/11/11679f410212ead297737ff3a6aaf5a8b2d8daba21abcb1199224857a14d3484.svg)\n\nis given by \n\n![](images/b7/b79eadc9e62263e563c3b385a3fe6dcb3eff75e9667cf9dbb7ccdb47c76ff12d.svg)\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{b}_i = \\mathbf{x}_i - \\mathbf{m}$ for $i = 1,2, \\ldots, n,$ and $\\mathbf{m}$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n![](images/3b/3bdd31917808dd86f0b247fba185f0f8f916412b9e86b1135ceb3a11f2c0ac1a.svg)\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n![](images/a7/a7d9c53005a25972fe250eafc83343434d213cb24ce883c1a6083966bcc39126.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e91708c9-67e2-5228-84fd-1f4ea60064ac" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mean-Deviation Form of an Observation Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1992f01f-9787-5991-8f07-c542a44b2013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512157} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6d92ea-4f1e-56e7-878a-54a61789a2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512160} {:answer/id #uuid "29bb63a6-1e69-50a0-8eec-34dab87c0ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512194} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc5a73a-1e0e-58fc-855d-8bc21714321a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512319} {:answer/id #uuid "b52309b5-e519-5c28-a0f7-1a897f42f5e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1992f01f-9787-5991-8f07-c542a44b2013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512157} :answer-field/id #uuid "84404952-a965-51e3-9ab4-430762ee3adc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22493cd3-4d2e-5c66-bb31-313c49c59d2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-137296" :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc84d72a-b9f3-5f99-aa23-31cfe6910d49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Covariance Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bcec93e-d7e4-55fc-819d-e39a0c56c36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512228} {:answer/id #uuid "66dc2bfe-a8d0-52a2-bc36-bab980d55b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 14\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512345} {:answer/id #uuid "ad38523d-e931-56dc-9284-35ef8fafb131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 14\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512507} {:answer/id #uuid "b62c6af3-3c8a-56d5-b2ac-1774ba25ab19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512532} {:answer/id #uuid "b933cfc5-7683-5ac9-83e0-5bc009cb9e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad38523d-e931-56dc-9284-35ef8fafb131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 14\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512507} :answer-field/id #uuid "77dc06de-331a-582a-93eb-90b96470671a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfee1079-b365-5e37-895d-1b49cb096f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-137297" :question/problem "$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $B$ in the mean-deviation form above, where the individual observations are given in columns. \nFind the sample covariance matrix of the data." :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 14\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc84d72a-b9f3-5f99-aa23-31cfe6910d49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Covariance Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23427465-7faa-5ff0-b334-b66b99b1e3d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}74 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512175} {:answer/id #uuid "585eb8e8-05fa-5f15-975e-62db16d4ca73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}34 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512302} {:answer/id #uuid "5e602860-7b01-5bf5-9eef-058a5b7a3b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}34 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512322} {:answer/id #uuid "a58cfba0-1623-53e1-b489-d1c2c499e821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}74 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512489} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bf6451-c2f0-513e-b209-8d9a57401167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}74 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23427465-7faa-5ff0-b334-b66b99b1e3d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}74 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512175} :answer-field/id #uuid "d42b2188-6c7d-56df-9ede-76ac26a0c9bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd71fe61-2928-5f70-8006-22add51dc115" :question/math-academy-id "q-137298" :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}74 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162094 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f930dfb2-a688-5dbe-9233-09dbcd2ba383" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sample Mean of an Observation Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e7cba67-e096-5b95-a14a-487e24567f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512323} {:answer/id #uuid "9235d87a-9ca8-57a7-b50d-33d634911d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512446} {:answer/id #uuid "a28ae8b8-c35f-5e4a-a510-e53932d48486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512477} {:answer/id #uuid "a8166e60-bcc2-5189-bbf7-7402566f0e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512501} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c38621-1b2b-5a28-b363-f6665e35d0c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{align